      <div class="band">

        <div class="c-1-6">
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <p class="body">Every piece of work travels the same circuit:</p>
          <ol class="doclist doclist--num">
            <li><strong>Acceptance criteria come first.</strong>
              <span>Before any agent starts, the ticket says exactly what
                &ldquo;done&rdquo; means. No criteria, no work.</span></li>
            <li><strong>A builder builds.</strong>
              <span>One specialist agent does the work and hands over the
                result &mdash; it doesn&rsquo;t grade itself.</span></li>
            <li><strong>A fresh critic reviews.</strong>
              <span>A separate agent, which never saw the builder&rsquo;s
                reasoning, judges the result against the criteria. If judging
                requires running the thing, it says so and demands
                proof.</span></li>
            <li><strong>A verifier runs it.</strong>
              <span>A third agent &mdash; never the builder &mdash; executes
                the work: runs the tests, loads the page, takes the
                screenshots, and records the results against the exact git
                commit.</span></li>
            <li><strong>You decide.</strong>
              <span>Approved work reaches you with its proof attached. You
                promote it or send it back.</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="coda">A critic answers with one of four words:
            <code class="verdict">APPROVED</code> (verified, not plausible),
            <code class="verdict">REVISE</code> (here&rsquo;s what&rsquo;s wrong),
            <code class="verdict">NEEDS_EVIDENCE</code> (run it and show me), or
            <code class="verdict">ESCALATE</code> (a human needs to look at
            this).</p>
          <p class="coda">Tickets from a tracker are mirrored into the repo as
            files, so every agent reads the same words you wrote &mdash; not a
            version retyped into a prompt along the way. Notes travel back to
            the tracker word for word.</p>
        </div>

